    {
      "data": {
        "text": "Implemented one of the most critical analytical mechanisms of IBM SPSS Statistics for sociology, polling, marketing research, and aggregated frequency data — the **Weight Cases** procedure (menu item `Data -> Weight Cases...`, engine command `data.weight_cases` / syntax `WEIGHT BY var.` / `WEIGHT OFF.`):"
      },
      "id": "blk_native_paragraph_1794063483751350100",
      "managed_by": "project_parser",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "data": {
        "text": "1. **Weighting Model in DataSet L1 (`statcraft_core::dataset`):**\n   - Added `weight_var: Option<String>` to `DataSet` accompanied by `set_weight_variable()`, `weight_var()`, `is_weighted()`, and `row_weight(row_idx)`;\n   - Enforced strict canonical SPSS rules: case weights must be non-negative ($w_i \\ge 0$), while cases with zero ($w_i = 0$), negative ($w_i < 0$), or missing values (`NaN` / `SYSMIS`) are systematically excluded from computations;\n   - Seamless synergy with `Select Cases`: when row filtering is active, weights are applied exclusively to active observations (`active_row_indices`), preventing statistical bias;\n   - Metadata synchronization: variable deletion (`remove_variable`) or renaming (`rename_variable`) safely updates or clears the weight state with zero panics."
      },
      "id": "blk_native_paragraph_1794063483751351100",
      "managed_by": "project_parser",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "data": {
        "text": "2. **Mathematical Core L2 (`statcraft_algo`):**\n   - **Frequencies (`frequencies.rs`):** weighted category frequencies ($f_w = \\sum w_i$), weighted valid and cumulative percentages, and weighted percentile/quartile distribution analysis;\n   - **Descriptive Statistics (`descriptives.rs`):** `compute_descriptives_weighted` routine computing weighted mean $\\bar{x}_w = \\frac{\\sum w_i x_i}{\\sum w_i}$, weighted sample variance $s_w^2 = \\frac{\\sum w_i (x_i - \\bar{x}_w)^2}{W - 1}$ (where $W = \\sum w_i$), standard error of the mean, weighted skewness, and weighted kurtosis;\n   - **Crosstabs (`crosstabs.rs`):** weighted $R \\times C$ contingency tables, marginal totals, and Pearson's Chi-Square test of independence incorporating frequency weights;\n   - 100% safe Rust, zero runtime overhead when weighting is disabled."
      },
